     "parsed_output": null,
     "text": "Round 1's `TILE=4096` (0.0218 ms) is about 88% of the measured 6539 GB/s peak (~19.2 \u00b5s for 120 MiB of traffic). Disabling TMA in round 5 changed nothing, so the instruction path is not the limit.\n\nThis round changes one variable, the tile size, from 4096 to 8192. That halves the grid to 2560 blocks, gives each block 16 KiB contiguous loads per operand, and keeps more bytes in flight per block. The gain is untested and may be within noise. The kernel is otherwise the same: one launch, fp16 add, zero-padded loads and clipped stores, so no element at index `>= n` is read or written.\n\n```python title=\"impl_cutile.py\"\nimport torch\nimport cuda.tile as ct\n\nConstInt = ct.Constant[int]\n\n# Fixed configuration literal: elements per block (fp16 -> 16 KiB per operand tile)\n_TILE = 8192\n\n\n@ct.kernel\ndef _vector_add_kernel(x, y, out, TILE: ConstInt):\n    bid = ct.bid(0)\n    # Bounds-padded loads for a possible partial last tile.\n    a = ct.load(x, (bid,), (TILE,), padding_mode=ct.PaddingMode.ZERO)\n    b = ct.load(y, (bid,), (TILE,), padding_mode=ct.PaddingMode.ZERO)\n    # Add in the input dtype (fp16), exactly like the reference.\n    c = a + b\n    # Bounds-clipped store: elements beyond n are dropped.\n    ct.store(out, (bid,), c)\n\n\ndef run(x, y):\n    out = torch.empty_like(x)\n    n = x.numel()\n    if n == 0:\n        return out\n    grid = (ct.cdiv(n, _TILE),)\n    ct.launch(torch.cuda.current_stream(), grid, _vector_add_kernel,\n              (x, y, out, _TILE))\n    return out\n\n\ndef get_last_config() -> dict:\n    return {\"TILE\": 8192}\n```",
     "type": "text"
    }
